    { "type": "textarea", "id": "disc_p1", "label": "Paragraph 1", "default": "I couldn't sleep anyway, so at 2am I did what nurses do when they're desperate — I started reading the actual studies. And it stopped me cold. A night sweat isn't a heat event at all. It begins in the hypothalamus, the brain's thermostat, which turns hypersensitive as estrogen swings through perimenopause. A tiny temperature change it used to ignore now trips the whole alarm: the flush, the sweat, the racing heart, the 2am soak. My body wasn't failing. Its thermostat had lost its buffer." },
